`rowt explain` for a NAME no rule matched, now that the router resolves such a
name (when the final route is direct) and lets the address rules route it. The
dig fixtures answer for the router's resolver — explain asks exactly it, with
the same `dig +short +time=2 +tries=1 @<server> <name>` both implementations
use for the captive check — so each case shows one outcome:

  corp-svc.example -> 198.51.100.7, inside the base fixture's corp CIDR
                      198.51.100.0/24                      -> CORP
  lan-svc.example  -> 10.20.30.40, private range           -> CORP (unbound)
  pub.example      -> 203.0.113.9                          -> DIRECT
  nores.example    -> no answer -> DIRECT, and a note with both hand fixes

With ROWT_FINAL=escape nothing is resolved and the trace has no dig; with
ROWT_PRIVATE_DEFAULT=direct the private name stays direct while the corp CIDR
still routes corp-svc.
